# Phase 18 Producer selector API audit — 2026-10-14T03:58:49Z

- P18-V01 audit of the pinned `DefaultMQProducerImpl` 82-method surface found that public `invokeMessageQueueSelector(Message, MessageQueueSelector, Object, long)` had no Rust standalone counterpart. The Java API is also reachable from the public producer through `getDefaultMQProducerImpl()` and is used by `DefaultMQProducer.send` selector overloads. The existing Rust selector-send methods covered selection only as part of sending.
- Added `Producer::invoke_message_queue_selector`, using a Rust closure to capture Java's `arg`. It validates the caller message, resolves current writable queues, passes logical queues and the caller's message to the selector, applies the namespace to the returned queue, enforces the timeout, and returns without sending. Existing selector-send paths now share the same queue transformation helper.
- Added the Java signature assertion against the pinned `producerImplementationApi` fixture and scripted-peer coverage for selector inputs, namespace result, null selection, timeout, unchanged caller message, and zero Broker sends. Updated the Producer compatibility matrix with the mapping and evidence.
- Initial regression compile failed as expected while the method was absent (and exposed that the test producer must be `Arc`-owned for `start`); after implementation, producer all-features tests passed: 119 unit tests, 10 admin, 16 producer, 3 request/reply, 13 scripted-send and 7 transaction integration tests. Strict producer Clippy, workspace formatting check, and `git diff --check` passed.
- This closes the standalone selector helper mapping only. P18-V01's broader overload/input/source-branch and Java reverse-export audit remains PARTIAL; full workspace P18-V02, P18-V03 package/examples checks, P18-V04 current Ubuntu CI and Phase 19 scenario execution/handoff remain open. Owner-run Windows/BSD/IPv6-only checks remain separate.
